/* Bassiet content, part 9: sites for the new strata (metabolism, antimicrobials, anaesthesia and analgesia,
   reproductive embryology, limbs and paediatric orthopaedics, child psychiatry and eating disorders,
   cranial nerves and the brainstem), their links, and practice items for every site that had none.
   Original wording. Links read as "from — label — to". Load after bank8.js. */
window.BANK = window.BANK || {C:[],E:[],CH:[],PT:[],CP:[]};
BANK.C.push(
/* Metabolism (The Furnace Ledgers) */
{id:'fatty-acids',n:'Fatty acids and ketone bodies',s:'General',L:{
 bio:'Synthesis runs in the cytosol: citrate carries acetyl-CoA out of mitochondria, acetyl-CoA carboxylase (biotin, switched on by insulin) makes malonyl-CoA in the rate-limiting step, and NADPH comes from the pentose phosphate pathway. Breakdown runs in mitochondria: long chains enter on the carnitine shuttle (CPT-1, which malonyl-CoA blocks) for β-oxidation.',
 phys:'In a long fast the liver turns surplus acetyl-CoA into acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate (HMG-CoA synthase). Brain, heart and muscle burn them; the liver lacks the transferase (thiophorase) to use them, and red cells have no mitochondria. The nitroprusside urine test sees acetoacetate, not β-hydroxybutyrate.',
 path:'MCAD deficiency: hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, vomiting and lethargy after a missed feed or illness, sometimes sudden infant death; dicarboxylic acids and C8 acylcarnitine are raised. Primary carnitine deficiency: weakness, cardiomyopathy and hypoketotic hypoglycaemia.',
 pharm:'Fat-oxidation defects are managed by never fasting and giving carbohydrate during illness. SGLT2 inhibitors can push insulin-poor patients into ketoacidosis with near-normal glucose.'}},
{id:'nucleotides',n:'Purine and pyrimidine synthesis',s:'General',L:{
 bio:'Purines are assembled on ribose-5-phosphate (from PRPP) using glycine, aspartate, glutamine and tetrahydrofolate. Pyrimidines build the ring first (carbamoyl phosphate synthetase II in the cytosol, then aspartate, orotate) and add PRPP afterwards. Ribonucleotide reductase makes deoxyribonucleotides; thymidylate synthase makes dTMP using folate that DHFR must regenerate.',
 path:'Lesch-Nyhan syndrome (HGPRT, X-linked recessive): salvage fails, PRPP and urate rise; gout, dystonia, self-biting and intellectual disability. ADA deficiency: toxic dATP blocks ribonucleotide reductase in lymphocytes, causing SCID. Orotic aciduria (UMP synthase): megaloblastic anaemia that B12 and folate do not fix, with normal ammonia.',
 pharm:'Methotrexate and trimethoprim (DHFR), 5-fluorouracil (thymidylate synthase), hydroxyurea (ribonucleotide reductase), mycophenolate and ribavirin (IMP dehydrogenase), leflunomide (dihydroorotate dehydrogenase), 6-mercaptopurine and azathioprine (de novo purine synthesis), allopurinol and febuxostat (xanthine oxidase). Uridine treats orotic aciduria.'}},
{id:'amino-acids',n:'Amino acid disorders',s:'General',L:{
 bio:'PKU: phenylalanine hydroxylase or its cofactor BH4 fails, so tyrosine becomes essential. Maple syrup urine disease: branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase (thiamine-dependent) cannot handle leucine, isoleucine and valine. Homocystinuria: usually cystathionine β-synthase deficiency. Alkaptonuria: homogentisate oxidase. Cystinuria: a shared kidney and gut transporter for cystine, ornithine, lysine and arginine. Hartnup: a neutral amino acid transporter that loses tryptophan.',
 path:'PKU: musty odour, fair skin, eczema, intellectual disability; maternal PKU damages the fetus. MSUD: sweet-smelling urine, poor feeding and encephalopathy in the first week. Homocystinuria: lens dislocated down and in, tall build, thromboses, intellectual disability. Alkaptonuria: urine darkens on standing, blue-black cartilage, early arthritis. Cystinuria: hexagonal stones. Hartnup: pellagra-like rash.',
 pharm:'PKU: low-phenylalanine diet with tyrosine, sapropterin for BH4-responsive forms. MSUD: restrict branched-chain amino acids, trial of thiamine. Homocystinuria: pyridoxine for responsive forms, betaine, low methionine. Cystinuria: fluids, urine alkalinisation, tiopronin or penicillamine.'}},
/* Antimicrobials (The Apothecary of Knives) */
{id:'cell-wall-drugs',n:'Cell-wall antibiotics',s:'Immune',L:{
 pharm:'β-lactams bind penicillin-binding proteins and stop transpeptidation: penicillin G (streptococci, syphilis), amoxicillin, antistaphylococcal penicillins (flucloxacillin, nafcillin), piperacillin, cephalosporins by generation, carbapenems (widest spectrum; imipenem lowers the seizure threshold) and aztreonam (Gram-negative only, usable in penicillin allergy). Vancomycin binds D-Ala-D-Ala on precursors: nephrotoxicity and flushing when infused fast.',
 micro:'Gram-negative outer membranes slow entry through porins. Ceftriaxone covers Neisseria and pneumococcus; ceftaroline covers MRSA; no cephalosporin reliably covers Listeria, enterococci or atypical organisms. Vancomycin misses all Gram-negatives.',
 bio:'The β-lactam ring mimics the D-Ala-D-Ala end of the peptide side chain. Clavulanate, sulbactam, tazobactam and avibactam inactivate β-lactamases so the partner drug survives.',
 path:'Rash and anaphylaxis (type I), immune haemolysis (type II), serum-sickness-like reactions (type III) and interstitial nephritis; ceftriaxone can sludge the gallbladder and displace bilirubin in newborns.'}},
{id:'ribosome-drugs',n:'Protein synthesis inhibitors',s:'Immune',L:{
 pharm:'30S: aminoglycosides (gentamicin, amikacin; bactericidal, need oxygen-dependent uptake so miss anaerobes; nephrotoxic, ototoxic, block the neuromuscular junction, teratogenic) and tetracyclines (doxycycline; chelated by calcium, iron and antacids; stain teeth, photosensitivity). 50S: macrolides (QT prolongation, motilin agonism; erythromycin and clarithromycin inhibit CYP3A4), clindamycin (anaerobes, C. difficile), linezolid (MRSA, VRE; thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome), chloramphenicol (aplastic anaemia, grey baby syndrome).',
 micro:'Organisms with no peptidoglycan or an intracellular life (Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella, Rickettsia) need tetracyclines or macrolides; doxycycline is used for Rocky Mountain spotted fever even in young children.',
 bio:'Bacterial ribosomes are 70S (30S + 50S), human cytosolic ribosomes 80S. Mitochondrial ribosomes resemble bacterial ones, which explains the marrow toxicity and lactic acidosis of linezolid and chloramphenicol.'}},
{id:'dna-folate-drugs',n:'Folate and DNA antibiotics',s:'Immune',L:{
 pharm:'Sulfonamides block dihydropteroate synthase and trimethoprim blocks bacterial DHFR; together (co-trimoxazole) they treat urinary infection and MRSA skin infection and prevent Pneumocystis and Toxoplasma. Fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) block DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Metronidazole makes free radicals that break DNA in anaerobes and protozoa. Nitrofurantoin is for lower urinary infection only.',
 path:'Sulfonamides: SJS and TEN, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, kernicterus in newborns, crystalluria. Trimethoprim: raised potassium (ENaC block, like amiloride) and creatinine (blocked secretion, not true GFR loss). Fluoroquinolones: tendon rupture (worse with steroids and age), QT prolongation, aortic aneurysm, cartilage injury in children. Metronidazole: disulfiram-like reaction, metallic taste, neuropathy.',
 micro:'Metronidazole: Giardia, Entamoeba, Trichomonas, bacterial vaginosis and anaerobes below the diaphragm. Ciprofloxacin covers Gram-negative rods including Pseudomonas; levofloxacin and moxifloxacin add respiratory pathogens.'}},
{id:'antimicrobial-resistance',n:'Antimicrobial resistance',s:'Immune',L:{
 micro:'MRSA: mecA makes PBP2a, which β-lactams barely bind. VRE: vanA swaps D-Ala-D-Ala for D-Ala-D-Lac. β-lactamases (penicillinases, ESBLs, carbapenemases such as KPC) open the ring. Aminoglycosides are acetylated, phosphorylated or adenylated; macrolides lose their 23S rRNA target to methylation or are pumped out; tetracyclines are pumped out; fluoroquinolones meet mutated gyrase.',
 bio:'Genes spread by transformation (naked DNA), transduction (phage) and conjugation (a plasmid passed through a pilus). Transposons jump between plasmid and chromosome and stack several resistance genes on one element.',
 pharm:'ESBL producers: carbapenems. Carbapenemase producers: ceftazidime-avibactam or polymyxins (colistin; nephrotoxic and neurotoxic). MRSA: vancomycin, daptomycin (not for pneumonia, surfactant inactivates it), linezolid, ceftaroline. VRE: linezolid or daptomycin.'}},
{id:'antivirals',n:'Antivirals and antiretrovirals',s:'Immune',L:{
 pharm:'Aciclovir and valaciclovir (HSV, VZV; need viral thymidine kinase), ganciclovir (CMV; activated by UL97; marrow suppression), foscarnet (pyrophosphate analogue needing no activation; nephrotoxic, low calcium and magnesium), cidofovir (nephrotoxic). Oseltamivir and zanamivir block neuraminidase. Hepatitis C: NS3/4A protease, NS5A and NS5B polymerase (sofosbuvir) inhibitors cure most. HIV: NRTIs, NNRTIs, integrase inhibitors, boosted protease inhibitors, entry blockers (maraviroc, enfuvirtide).',
 path:'Zidovudine: marrow suppression. Abacavir: hypersensitivity, so test HLA-B*57:01 first. Older tenofovir: kidney and bone loss. Efavirenz: vivid dreams and mood change. Protease inhibitors: lipodystrophy and hyperglycaemia. NRTIs can cause lactic acidosis through mitochondrial toxicity. Aciclovir crystallises in the tubules if the patient is dry.',
 micro:'Aciclovir-resistant HSV usually lacks thymidine kinase, so foscarnet still works; UL97 mutant CMV resists ganciclovir.'}},
{id:'antimycobacterials',n:'Antimycobacterial drugs and leprosy',s:'Immune',L:{
 pharm:'Rifampicin blocks bacterial RNA polymerase: orange urine and tears, strong CYP inducer (warfarin, the pill, HIV drugs fail). Isoniazid blocks mycolic acid synthesis after KatG activation: hepatitis, neuropathy prevented by pyridoxine, drug-induced lupus. Pyrazinamide: hepatitis, high urate. Ethambutol blocks arabinosyltransferase: optic neuritis with red-green colour loss. Leprosy: dapsone with rifampicin, plus clofazimine for multibacillary disease; dapsone haemolyses in G6PD deficiency.',
 micro:'M. leprae prefers cool tissue (skin, nerves, nose) and cannot be cultured; armadillos carry it. Tuberculoid leprosy: strong Th1 response, few bacilli, a few numb plaques. Lepromatous: weak cell-mediated (Th2-leaning) response, many bacilli, leonine face, glove-and-stocking loss. M. avium complex in AIDS below 50 CD4 cells: a macrolide plus ethambutol. M. marinum: fish-tank granulomas.',
 bio:'Slow NAT2 acetylators keep isoniazid longer and have more toxicity.'}},
/* Anaesthesia and analgesia (The Sleeping Draughts) */
{id:'general-anaesthetics',n:'General anaesthetics',s:'General',L:{
 pharm:'Inhaled: sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane, nitrous oxide. IV: propofol (quick clear-headed recovery, antiemetic; hypotension, propofol infusion syndrome), ketamine (NMDA antagonist; keeps blood pressure and airway reflexes, bronchodilates; emergence hallucinations), etomidate (cardiovascularly stable; suppresses 11β-hydroxylase), thiopental, midazolam, dexmedetomidine (α₂ agonist).',
 phys:'Potency = 1/MAC, the alveolar concentration that stops movement to incision in half of patients; high oil:gas solubility means a low MAC. Speed of induction and recovery depends on low blood:gas solubility (desflurane and nitrous oxide are fastest). Volatiles lower blood pressure, depress breathing and the CO₂ response, relax smooth muscle including the uterus, and raise cerebral blood flow while cutting cerebral metabolism.',
 path:'Volatiles and succinylcholine trigger malignant hyperthermia in RYR1 carriers. Halothane caused hepatitis. Nitrous oxide expands closed gas spaces (pneumothorax, bowel) and inactivates B12.'}},
{id:'local-anaesthetics',n:'Local anaesthetics',s:'General',L:{
 pharm:'Esters (procaine, tetracaine, benzocaine; split by plasma esterases; more allergy through PABA) and amides (lidocaine, bupivacaine, ropivacaine, prilocaine; liver metabolism). They block voltage-gated Na⁺ channels from inside the cell, binding best to open and inactivated channels. Adrenaline added to the injection slows absorption and prolongs the block.',
 phys:'Only the uncharged base crosses the membrane, so acidic, infected tissue weakens the block. Small fibres block first: pain and temperature go before touch, pressure and motor power, and return in reverse.',
 path:'Systemic toxicity: perioral tingling, tinnitus, agitation and seizures, then arrhythmia and collapse; bupivacaine is the most cardiotoxic. Treat with lipid emulsion. Prilocaine and benzocaine can cause methaemoglobinaemia.'}},
{id:'nmj-blockers',n:'Neuromuscular blockers',s:'General',L:{
 pharm:'Depolarising: succinylcholine holds the end plate depolarised (phase I: fasciculation then flaccid paralysis, made worse by cholinesterase inhibitors; phase II with prolonged use: desensitised, partly reversible). Non-depolarising (rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium) compete with acetylcholine; reverse with neostigmine plus glycopyrrolate, or rocuronium and vecuronium with sugammadex, which encapsulates them.',
 path:'Succinylcholine dangers: hyperkalaemia after burns, crush, denervation or long immobility (extra receptors spread across the muscle), malignant hyperthermia, bradycardia, hours of apnoea with pseudocholinesterase deficiency.',
 phys:'Atracurium and cisatracurium break down spontaneously in plasma (Hofmann elimination), so they suit liver or kidney failure. Myasthenia patients are very sensitive to non-depolarising blockers and resistant to succinylcholine.'}},
{id:'opioids',n:'Opioids',s:'Neuro',L:{
 pharm:'μ agonists (morphine, fentanyl, oxycodone, codeine, methadone) open K⁺ channels and close presynaptic Ca²⁺ channels, cutting release of glutamate and substance P. Buprenorphine is a partial agonist with a ceiling on respiratory depression; naloxone (short) and naltrexone (long) are antagonists. Loperamide stays in the gut; tramadol and pethidine add serotonergic effects (seizures, serotonin syndrome); methadone prolongs QT.',
 path:'Overdose: pinpoint pupils, slow shallow breathing, coma. Tolerance develops to analgesia, euphoria and respiratory depression but not to constipation or miosis. Codeine becomes morphine through CYP2D6, so ultra-rapid metabolisers can overdose; morphine metabolites build up in kidney failure.',
 phys:'Opioids act in the dorsal horn, the periaqueductal grey and the rostral ventromedial medulla, and blunt the brainstem response to CO₂.'}},
{id:'analgesics',n:'NSAIDs and paracetamol',s:'General',L:{
 pharm:'Ibuprofen, naproxen and diclofenac block COX-1 and COX-2 reversibly; aspirin acetylates COX irreversibly, so platelets, which cannot make new enzyme, stay blocked for their 7–10-day life. Celecoxib spares COX-1. Paracetamol acts mainly in the brain and has little anti-inflammatory effect.',
 path:'NSAIDs: peptic ulcer, afferent arteriolar constriction and AKI, interstitial nephritis, fluid retention, aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease; COX-2 selectivity raises MI and stroke risk. Aspirin: Reye syndrome in children with viral illness; overdose causes tinnitus and a mixed respiratory alkalosis with anion gap acidosis.',
 phys:'COX-1 keeps gastric mucus and renal blood flow going; COX-2 is induced by inflammation. Platelet thromboxane A₂ and endothelial prostacyclin balance each other.',
 bio:'Paracetamol overdose exhausts glutathione so NAPQI kills zone 3 hepatocytes; N-acetylcysteine refills glutathione.'}},
/* Reproductive embryology (The First Folds) */
{id:'gametogenesis',n:'Gametogenesis and meiosis',s:'Repro',L:{
 bio:'Meiosis I separates homologous chromosomes (46 to 23) after crossing over in prophase I; meiosis II separates sister chromatids. Non-disjunction in meiosis I gives a gamete with one copy of each parental homologue; in meiosis II, two identical copies.',
 phys:'Oogonia enter meiosis before birth and arrest in prophase I as primary oocytes until puberty. Each cycle one completes meiosis I at the LH surge and arrests in metaphase II; meiosis II finishes only if a sperm enters. Spermatogenesis begins at puberty and runs continuously for about two months per cycle: spermatogonia, primary and secondary spermatocytes, spermatids, spermatozoa, nursed by Sertoli cells behind the blood-testis barrier.',
 path:'Non-disjunction rises with maternal age; most trisomy 21 arises in maternal meiosis I. Primary ciliary dyskinesia (dynein arm defect): immotile sperm, bronchiectasis, situs inversus in half. Cryptorchidism impairs spermatogenesis and raises germ cell tumour risk.'}},
{id:'sex-development',n:'Sexual differentiation and its disorders',s:'Repro',L:{
 phys:'SRY on the Y chromosome turns the gonad into a testis. Sertoli cells make anti-Müllerian hormone, which regresses the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts; Leydig testosterone keeps the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts as epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles and ejaculatory ducts; DHT builds the penis, scrotum and prostate. Without SRY the Müllerian ducts form tubes, uterus and upper vagina and the Wolffian ducts fade. Homologues: genital tubercle to glans penis or clitoris; urogenital folds to ventral penis or labia minora; labioscrotal swellings to scrotum or labia majora.',
 path:'Androgen insensitivity (46,XY, androgen receptor): female external genitalia, undescended testes, no uterus, sparse pubic hair, high testosterone and LH. 5α-reductase deficiency (46,XY): ambiguous genitalia that virilise at puberty. 21-hydroxylase CAH in a 46,XX baby: virilised genitalia with ovaries and uterus. Müllerian agenesis: normal ovaries and breasts, no uterus or upper vagina. Incomplete Müllerian fusion: bicornuate or septate uterus. Wolffian remnant in women: Gartner duct cyst.',
 pharm:'Finasteride and dutasteride are teratogenic to a male fetus; pregnant women should not handle broken tablets.'}},
/* Limbs and paediatric orthopaedics (The Frame) */
{id:'upper-limb',n:'Upper limb nerves and injuries',s:'MSK & skin',L:{
 path:'Upper trunk C5–C6 (Erb palsy: shoulder dystocia, fall on the shoulder): arm hangs adducted and internally rotated with the elbow extended and forearm pronated. Lower trunk C8–T1 (Klumpke: grabbing a branch while falling, Pancoast tumour): claw hand, sometimes Horner. Long thoracic (axillary surgery): winged scapula. Axillary (surgical neck fracture, dislocation): weak deltoid, numb lateral shoulder. Radial (midshaft humerus): wrist drop. Median (supracondylar fracture, carpal tunnel): thenar wasting, numb lateral 3½ fingers. Ulnar (medial epicondyle, hook of hamate): clawing of fingers 4 and 5, Froment sign.',
 phys:'Rotator cuff: supraspinatus starts abduction (0–15°) and tears most often; infraspinatus and teres minor rotate outward; subscapularis rotates inward. Deltoid abducts to 90°, then trapezius and serratus anterior rotate the scapula. Lumbricals flex the MCP joints and extend the IP joints; dorsal interossei abduct, palmar interossei adduct.',
 micro:'Scaphoid fracture after a fall on the outstretched hand: snuffbox tenderness, and blood enters distally, so the proximal pole can die (avascular necrosis). Boxer fracture: fifth metacarpal neck.'}},
{id:'lower-limb',n:'Lower limb nerves and knee injuries',s:'MSK & skin',L:{
 path:'Common peroneal (fibular neck fracture, tight cast, crossing legs): foot drop, steppage gait, numb dorsum of the foot. Tibial (knee trauma, tarsal tunnel): cannot plantarflex or stand on tiptoe, numb sole. Femoral (pelvic fracture, haematoma): weak knee extension, lost knee jerk. Obturator (pelvic surgery): weak adduction. Superior gluteal (misplaced injection): Trendelenburg sign, the pelvis dropping on the opposite side when standing on the affected leg. Inferior gluteal: difficulty rising from a chair or climbing stairs.',
 phys:'Anterior drawer and Lachman test the ACL; posterior drawer the PCL; valgus stress the MCL; varus stress the LCL; McMurray and joint-line tenderness the menisci. A lateral blow to a planted knee classically tears ACL, MCL and the medial meniscus, though the lateral meniscus is often torn in acute injuries.'}},
{id:'paediatric-ortho',n:'Paediatric orthopaedics',s:'MSK & skin',L:{
 path:'Developmental dysplasia of the hip: breech, girls, first-born, family history; Ortolani and Barlow tests, ultrasound in early infancy, Pavlik harness. Legg-Calvé-Perthes (4–10 years): idiopathic avascular necrosis of the femoral head with a limp and mild pain. Slipped capital femoral epiphysis (obese adolescent; hypothyroidism, growth hormone): hip or knee pain, the leg rotates outward as the hip flexes; surgical pinning. Osgood-Schlatter: traction apophysitis of the tibial tubercle in sporty adolescents. Pulled elbow: radial head subluxation after a tug on the arm. Septic arthritis: fever, refusal to bear weight, high CRP; washout.',
 bio:'Osteogenesis imperfecta: type I collagen (COL1A1/COL1A2), blue sclerae, brittle bones, hearing loss, an important mimic of abuse. Achondroplasia: activating FGFR3 mutation, autosomal dominant, usually new and linked to older fathers.',
 phys:'Growth plates are weaker than ligaments in children, so an injury that sprains an adult fractures a child through the physis (Salter-Harris types).'}},
/* Child psychiatry and eating disorders (The Small Rooms) */
{id:'adhd',n:'ADHD and tic disorders',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'ADHD: inattention and/or hyperactivity-impulsivity, with several symptoms before age 12, present in two or more settings and impairing function; it often persists into adulthood. Tourette syndrome: multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic for over a year, starting before 18; often with OCD and ADHD.',
 pharm:'Stimulants (methylphenidate, amphetamines) raise synaptic dopamine and noradrenaline: poor appetite, insomnia, slowed growth, raised heart rate. Atomoxetine blocks noradrenaline reuptake; guanfacine and clonidine are α₂ agonists that also quieten tics. Tics: habit-reversal therapy, α₂ agonists, then antipsychotics such as aripiprazole.',
 phys:'Prefrontal-striatal circuits using dopamine and noradrenaline hold attention; tics come from cortico-striato-thalamic loops.'}},
{id:'autism',n:'Autism and intellectual disability',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Autism spectrum disorder: persistent deficits in social communication plus restricted, repetitive behaviour or interests (including unusual sensory reactions) from early childhood; diagnosed about four times more often in boys; often with intellectual disability or epilepsy. Rett syndrome (MECP2, X-linked, almost only girls): normal early months, then lost speech and purposeful hand use, hand-wringing, slowing head growth. Intellectual disability: limited intellectual and adaptive function beginning in childhood.',
 bio:'Fragile X (FMR1 CGG expansion; methylation silences the gene) is the commonest inherited cause of intellectual disability and a frequent cause of autism. Down syndrome is the commonest genetic cause overall; tuberous sclerosis brings autism with seizures.',
 pharm:'Behavioural and educational therapy treat the core features; risperidone or aripiprazole can reduce severe irritability and aggression.'}},
{id:'childhood-behaviour',n:'Conduct, oppositional and separation disorders',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Oppositional defiant disorder: angry, argumentative, vindictive behaviour toward authority for at least 6 months, without serious violation of others\' rights. Conduct disorder: repeated violation of others\' rights or major rules (aggression to people or animals, destruction, theft, truancy) over 12 months; after 18 it may become antisocial personality disorder. Separation anxiety disorder: excessive fear of separation from attachment figures, school refusal and physical complaints. Disruptive mood dysregulation disorder: severe outbursts on a persistently irritable mood, onset before 10, diagnosed between 6 and 18.',
 pharm:'Parent management training and family therapy come first; CBT, then SSRIs, for childhood anxiety; stimulants help when ADHD coexists.'}},
{id:'eating-disorders',n:'Eating disorders',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Anorexia nervosa: restriction, intense fear of weight gain, distorted body image and low body weight (restricting or binge-purge type). Bulimia nervosa: binges with compensation (vomiting, laxatives, fasting, exercise) at least weekly for 3 months, usually at normal weight. Binge-eating disorder: binges with distress but no compensation, often obesity. ARFID: avoidant or restrictive eating from sensory dislike or fear, without body-image disturbance.',
 phys:'Anorexia: low GnRH and amenorrhoea, low oestrogen and osteoporosis, bradycardia, hypotension, hypothermia, lanugo, low T3 with normal TSH, raised cortisol and growth hormone. Bulimia: parotid swelling, dental erosion, calluses on the knuckles, hypokalaemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis.',
 bio:'Refeeding syndrome: the insulin surge drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells; hypophosphataemia starves cells of ATP, causing heart failure, arrhythmia, weakness and seizures.',
 pharm:'Anorexia: supervised refeeding and family-based therapy; olanzapine can help weight gain. Bulimia: CBT and fluoxetine. Binge-eating: CBT, lisdexamfetamine. Bupropion is contraindicated in bulimia and anorexia because it lowers the seizure threshold.'}},
{id:'personality-disorders',n:'Personality disorders',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Enduring, inflexible patterns from adolescence or early adulthood. Cluster A (odd): paranoid, schizoid, schizotypal (magical thinking, odd speech). Cluster B (dramatic): antisocial (conduct disorder before 15, diagnosed from 18), borderline (unstable mood, relationships and identity, self-harm, splitting), histrionic, narcissistic. Cluster C (anxious): avoidant, dependent, obsessive-compulsive personality (perfectionism felt as right, unlike the unwanted thoughts of OCD).',
 pharm:'Psychotherapy is the core: dialectical behaviour therapy for borderline personality disorder. Medicines only target symptoms such as mood swings or impulsivity.'}},
/* Cranial nerves and the brainstem (The Twelve Wires) */
{id:'cranial-nerves',n:'Cranial nerves',s:'Neuro',L:{
 phys:'Nuclei: III and IV in the midbrain; V, VI, VII and VIII in the pons; IX, X and XII in the medulla, XI partly in the cervical cord. Motor nuclei lie medial and sensory nuclei lateral. Exits: superior orbital fissure (III, IV, V1, VI), foramen rotundum (V2), foramen ovale (V3), internal acoustic meatus (VII, VIII), jugular foramen (IX, X, XI), hypoglossal canal (XII).',
 path:'V: jaw deviates toward a weak pterygoid; trigeminal neuralgia. VII: a lower motor neurone lesion (Bell palsy) weakens the whole half-face with hyperacusis and lost taste; an upper motor neurone lesion spares the forehead. IX and X: uvula deviates away from the lesion, lost gag. XI: weak shrug, weak head turn away from the lesion. XII: tongue deviates toward the lesion.',
 micro:'Facial palsy follows Lyme disease (often both sides) and VZV of the geniculate ganglion (Ramsay Hunt: ear vesicles); HSV-1 is linked to Bell palsy.',
 pharm:'Carbamazepine for trigeminal neuralgia; prednisolone within 72 hours for Bell palsy.'}},
{id:'brainstem',n:'Brainstem syndromes',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Medial medullary (anterior spinal artery): contralateral hemiparesis and proprioception loss, tongue deviating to the lesion. Lateral medullary (PICA or vertebral): hoarseness and dysphagia (nucleus ambiguus), ipsilateral facial and contralateral body pain-temperature loss, ipsilateral Horner, ataxia, vertigo. Lateral pontine (AICA): ipsilateral facial paralysis and deafness, ataxia. Medial midbrain (Weber): ipsilateral CN III palsy with contralateral hemiparesis. Ventral pons (basilar): locked-in syndrome with vertical eye movements and blinking kept. MLF: internuclear ophthalmoplegia.',
 phys:'Cranial nerve signs are ipsilateral and long-tract signs contralateral, so a crossed pattern places the lesion at the level of the affected nerve.'}},
{id:'cortical-syndromes',n:'Aphasias and cortical syndromes',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Broca (inferior frontal gyrus): non-fluent, effortful speech, comprehension kept, repetition poor. Wernicke (superior temporal gyrus): fluent, empty speech, poor comprehension and repetition, little insight. Conduction (arcuate fasciculus): fluent, comprehension kept, repetition poor. Global: all lost. Transcortical forms keep repetition. Dominant parietal (angular gyrus): Gerstmann syndrome (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right confusion). Non-dominant parietal: hemineglect. Bilateral amygdala: Klüver-Bucy (hyperorality, hypersexuality, placidity). Prefrontal: disinhibition, apathy, poor judgement.',
 phys:'Most people, including most left-handers, are left-hemisphere dominant for language. Broca and Wernicke areas lie in the superior and inferior divisions of the left MCA territory.'}},
{id:'herniation',n:'Brain herniation and raised pressure',s:'Neuro',L:{
 path:'Subfalcine (cingulate) herniation compresses the anterior cerebral artery: leg weakness. Uncal herniation: fixed, dilated ipsilateral pupil (CN III), contralateral hemiparesis, or ipsilateral weakness when the opposite peduncle is pushed against the tentorium (Kernohan notch); the PCA can be pinched. Central downward herniation tears pontine perforators (Duret haemorrhages). Tonsillar herniation through the foramen magnum compresses the medulla: apnoea and death.',
 phys:'Cerebral perfusion pressure = MAP − ICP. Brain, blood and CSF share a fixed skull volume (Monro-Kellie). The Cushing reflex (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) signals brainstem compression.',
 pharm:'Raise the head of the bed, hyperventilate briefly (lower PaCO₂ constricts cerebral vessels), mannitol or hypertonic saline; dexamethasone for oedema around tumours, not for head injury.'}}
);

