BANK.CH.push(
/* Chains for sites that had no practice item */
{id:'ch_apoptosis',t:'The intrinsic path to apoptosis',sub:['bio','path'],sys:'General',c:['apoptosis','oncogenes'],
 steps:['DNA damage or loss of growth signals activates BH3-only proteins','BAX and BAK open pores in the outer mitochondrial membrane','Cytochrome c leaks into the cytosol','Cytochrome c and APAF-1 assemble the apoptosome','Caspase-9 activates executioner caspases 3 and 7','The cell shrinks and buds into membrane-bound apoptotic bodies','Macrophages eat the bodies without inflammation'],
 pearl:'BCL-2 holds BAX and BAK shut; t(14;18) overexpresses it in follicular lymphoma, and venetoclax prises it away. The extrinsic route (FAS ligand, caspase-8) joins at the executioner caspases.'},
{id:'ch_hfspiral',t:'The neurohormonal spiral of heart failure',sub:['phys','pharm'],sys:'Cardio',c:['heart-failure','raas'],
 steps:['Damaged myocardium ejects a smaller stroke volume','Baroreceptors sense falling arterial pressure','Sympathetic tone and renin release rise','Angiotensin II and aldosterone retain salt and water','Preload and afterload rise and the ventricle remodels','Wall stress climbs and contractility falls further'],
 pearl:'Drugs that cut this loop (ACE inhibitor or ARNI, β-blocker, mineralocorticoid antagonist, SGLT2 inhibitor) prolong life; loop diuretics only relieve congestion.'},
{id:'ch_emphysema',t:'Smoking to emphysema',sub:['path','phys'],sys:'Resp',c:['copd','leukocyte-migration'],
 steps:['Smoke recruits neutrophils and macrophages to the alveoli','Neutrophils release elastase','Elastase outweighs α₁-antitrypsin, which smoke oxidants also inactivate','Elastic fibres in alveolar walls are digested','Airspaces enlarge and elastic recoil is lost','Small airways collapse on expiration and air is trapped'],
 pearl:'Smoking destroys the centre of the acinus in the upper lobes; α₁-antitrypsin deficiency destroys whole acini in the lower lobes, often before 45.'},
{id:'ch_nrds',t:'Neonatal respiratory distress',sub:['phys','path'],sys:'Resp',c:['surfactant'],
 steps:['Type II pneumocytes of a preterm lung make too little surfactant','Alveolar surface tension stays high','Small alveoli collapse at the end of each breath','Collapsed units are perfused but not ventilated, causing shunt','Hypoxaemia injures capillaries, which leak protein','The protein forms hyaline membranes'],
 pearl:'Antenatal corticosteroids speed surfactant production; maternal diabetes delays it because fetal insulin suppresses it. A lecithin:sphingomyelin ratio above 2 means mature lungs.'},
{id:'ch_lobar',t:'Lobar pneumonia',sub:['micro','path'],sys:'Resp',c:['pneumonia','gram-positive-cocci'],
 steps:['Pneumococci are aspirated into a lobe','The capsule resists phagocytosis and bacteria multiply','Congestion: vessels dilate and alveoli fill with fluid and bacteria','Red hepatisation: red cells, neutrophils and fibrin fill the alveoli','Grey hepatisation: red cells break down, leaving fibrin and neutrophils','Resolution: enzymes digest the exudate'],
 pearl:'The consolidated lobe is dull to percussion with bronchial breathing; perfusion of unventilated alveoli causes shunt. Pneumococcal vaccines target the capsule.'},
{id:'ch_ascites',t:'Portal hypertension to ascites',sub:['phys','path'],sys:'GI & liver',c:['cirrhosis','raas'],
 steps:['Fibrosis and nodules raise resistance to flow through the liver','Portal venous pressure rises','Splanchnic arterioles dilate under nitric oxide','Effective arterial volume falls','RAAS, sympathetic tone and ADH retain sodium and water','Fluid weeps from congested sinusoids into the peritoneum'],
 pearl:'A serum-ascites albumin gradient of 1.1 g/dL or more points to portal hypertension. Spironolactone blocks the aldosterone step; varices, splenomegaly and caput medusae come from the same pressure.'},
{id:'ch_cfairway',t:'A CFTR defect in the airway',sub:['bio','phys','micro'],sys:'Resp',c:['cystic-fibrosis'],
 steps:['ΔF508 CFTR misfolds and is destroyed in the endoplasmic reticulum','Little CFTR reaches the apical membrane','Airway cells secrete less chloride and absorb more sodium','The airway surface liquid dehydrates and mucus thickens','Cilia cannot clear the mucus','Chronic infection (S. aureus, then Pseudomonas) causes bronchiectasis'],
 pearl:'In sweat ducts the same defect blocks chloride reabsorption, so sweat is salty. Elexacaftor-tezacaftor-ivacaftor helps the protein fold and holds the channel open.'},
{id:'ch_ureacycle',t:'The urea cycle',sub:['bio'],sys:'GI & liver',c:['ammonia','nucleotides'],
 steps:['Ammonia and bicarbonate join as carbamoyl phosphate (CPS I, needs N-acetylglutamate)','Carbamoyl phosphate and ornithine make citrulline (OTC)','Citrulline leaves the mitochondrion and joins aspartate','Argininosuccinate splits into arginine and fumarate','Arginase cleaves arginine into urea and ornithine','Ornithine returns to the mitochondrion for the next turn'],
 pearl:'OTC deficiency (X-linked) dumps carbamoyl phosphate into pyrimidine synthesis, so orotic acid rises with ammonia; CPS I deficiency raises ammonia without orotic acid; orotic aciduria proper has normal ammonia.'},
{id:'ch_meningitis',t:'Bacterial meningitis',sub:['micro','path'],sys:'Neuro',c:['meningitis','herniation'],
 steps:['Bacteria colonise the nasopharynx','They invade the bloodstream','They cross the blood-brain barrier into the subarachnoid space','Cell wall fragments trigger cytokines and a neutrophil influx','Vessels leak and brain oedema raises intracranial pressure','Headache, neck stiffness, confusion and seizures follow'],
 pearl:'CSF shows neutrophils, high protein and low glucose. Dexamethasone given with or just before the first antibiotic dose blunts the inflammatory burst (proven for pneumococcus).'},
{id:'ch_g6pdcrisis',t:'Oxidant haemolysis in G6PD deficiency',sub:['bio','pharm'],sys:'Blood & cancer',c:['g6pd','dna-folate-drugs'],
 steps:['Low G6PD activity limits NADPH from the pentose phosphate pathway','An oxidant arrives: primaquine, dapsone, a sulfonamide, fava beans or infection','Reduced glutathione is used up and cannot be regenerated','Haemoglobin oxidises and precipitates as Heinz bodies','Splenic macrophages bite the inclusions out, leaving bite cells','Damaged cells haemolyse: anaemia, dark urine and jaundice'],
 pearl:'A G6PD test during a crisis can look falsely normal because the oldest, most deficient cells have already gone; retest after recovery.'},
{id:'ch_methotrexate',t:'How methotrexate stops DNA synthesis',sub:['pharm','bio'],sys:'Blood & cancer',c:['chemo','nucleotides'],
 steps:['Methotrexate binds dihydrofolate reductase','Dihydrofolate cannot be reduced to tetrahydrofolate','Thymidylate synthase runs out of methylene-tetrahydrofolate','dTMP and purine synthesis stall','Dividing cells arrest in S phase','Marrow, gut lining and mouth suffer first'],
 pearl:'Leucovorin (folinic acid) bypasses the block to rescue normal cells; 5-fluorouracil acts one step later, on thymidylate synthase itself.'},
{id:'ch_syphilis',t:'Untreated syphilis',sub:['micro','path'],sys:'Repro',c:['sti','spinal-cord'],
 steps:['Treponema pallidum enters through a break in the mucosa','A painless chancre forms at the site (primary)','Spirochaetes spread: rash on palms and soles, condylomata lata (secondary)','Lesions heal and the infection becomes latent','Years later endarteritis causes gummas, aortitis and tabes dorsalis (tertiary)'],
 pearl:'Tabes removes the dorsal columns: positive Romberg, and Argyll Robertson pupils that accommodate but do not react to light. Penicillin G treats every stage; expect a Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction.'},
{id:'ch_goutattack',t:'An acute gout attack',sub:['path','bio'],sys:'MSK & skin',c:['gout-purines','nucleotides'],
 steps:['Serum urate stays above its solubility limit','Urate crystals form in a cool peripheral joint such as the big toe','Synovial macrophages engulf the crystals','The NLRP3 inflammasome releases IL-1β','Neutrophils pour in and the joint becomes hot, red and swollen'],
 pearl:'Needle-shaped, negatively birefringent crystals. Treat the attack with an NSAID, colchicine or a steroid; lower urate later with allopurinol or febuxostat under anti-inflammatory cover.'},
{id:'ch_barrett',t:'Reflux to adenocarcinoma',sub:['path'],sys:'GI & liver',c:['cell-adaptation','oncogenes'],
 steps:['Acid reflux repeatedly injures the lower oesophagus','Squamous epithelium is replaced by columnar epithelium with goblet cells','Ongoing injury produces low-grade dysplasia','Low-grade dysplasia progresses to high-grade dysplasia','Adenocarcinoma invades the wall'],
 pearl:'Metaplasia can reverse if the stimulus stops; dysplasia is the step that predicts cancer, which is why Barrett oesophagus is kept under surveillance.'},
{id:'ch_alamyloid',t:'AL amyloidosis',sub:['path'],sys:'General',c:['amyloidosis','myeloma'],
 steps:['A plasma cell clone makes excess free light chains','The light chains misfold into β-pleated sheets','Fibrils deposit outside cells in many organs','Organs stiffen and fail: restrictive heart, nephrotic kidney, large tongue, neuropathy'],
 pearl:'Congo red stains the deposits, which glow apple-green under polarised light; an abdominal fat pad biopsy is a simple first test. AA amyloid comes from serum amyloid A in chronic inflammation.'},
{id:'ch_lynch',t:'Mismatch repair failure',sub:['bio','path'],sys:'GI & liver',c:['dna-repair','colorectal-cancer'],
 steps:['A person inherits one faulty mismatch repair gene such as MLH1 or MSH2','The second copy is lost in a colonic stem cell','Replication slips in repetitive DNA are no longer repaired','Microsatellites change length','Mutations pile up in growth-control genes','A right-sided colon cancer appears early, from few polyps'],
 pearl:'Lynch syndrome also raises endometrial, ovarian and urothelial cancer risk. MSI-high tumours carry many neoantigens and respond to checkpoint inhibitors.'},
{id:'ch_anticipation',t:'Anticipation in Huntington disease',sub:['bio','path'],sys:'General',c:['repeat-expansion','genetic-disorders'],
 steps:['A father carries an expanded CAG repeat in HTT','The repeat grows further during spermatogenesis','His child inherits a longer repeat','A longer polyglutamine tract makes huntingtin toxic sooner','Caudate neurons die earlier in life','Chorea, mood change and dementia begin at a younger age'],
 pearl:'Huntington expands mainly through the father; fragile X and congenital myotonic dystrophy expand mainly through the mother.'},
{id:'ch_steadystate',t:'Reaching steady state',sub:['pharm'],sys:'General',c:['pharmacokinetics'],
 steps:['A drug is started at a fixed dose each dosing interval','Each dose adds drug while elimination removes a fixed fraction','After one half-life the level is half of its final value','After 4–5 half-lives intake matches elimination','The steady-state level is set by dose rate and clearance'],
 pearl:'A loading dose (target concentration × Vd) skips the wait. Changing the dose changes the steady-state level, not the time it takes to get there.'},
{id:'ch_classswitch',t:'Switching from IgM to IgG',sub:['phys'],sys:'Immune',c:['antibodies','mhc-tcells'],
 steps:['A naive B cell binds antigen with surface IgM','It takes the antigen in and presents peptide on MHC II','A helper T cell recognises the peptide and expresses CD40L','CD40 signalling with cytokines triggers class switching','The switched B cell matures into a plasma cell secreting IgG'],
 pearl:'Without CD40L (hyper-IgM syndrome) IgM stays high while IgG, IgA and IgE are almost absent; Pneumocystis and Cryptosporidium take advantage.'},
{id:'ch_acuterejection',t:'Acute cellular rejection',sub:['path','pharm'],sys:'Immune',c:['transplant','mhc-tcells'],
 steps:['A kidney with mismatched HLA is transplanted','Donor dendritic cells carry graft antigens to lymph nodes','Recipient T cells recognise the foreign MHC','Calcineurin signalling switches on IL-2','CD8 T cells expand and infiltrate the graft','Tubules and vessels are damaged weeks to months later'],
 pearl:'Tacrolimus and ciclosporin block calcineurin; sirolimus blocks mTOR downstream of IL-2; basiliximab blocks the IL-2 receptor.'},
{id:'ch_strongyloides',t:'Strongyloides hyperinfection',sub:['micro'],sys:'Immune',c:['helminths','gram-negatives'],
 steps:['Larvae in soil penetrate bare skin','They travel to the lungs and are coughed up and swallowed','Adults in the small bowel lay eggs that hatch there','Some larvae re-invade through the gut wall (autoinfection)','Steroids suppress immunity and autoinfection runs away','Larvae carry gut bacteria into the blood: Gram-negative sepsis and meningitis'],
 pearl:'Treat with ivermectin, and screen or treat people from endemic regions before high-dose steroids.'},
{id:'ch_renalcrisis',t:'Scleroderma renal crisis',sub:['path','pharm'],sys:'MSK & skin',c:['connective-tissue','hypertension'],
 steps:['Autoimmune injury damages small arteries in diffuse scleroderma','Intimal thickening narrows the interlobular arteries','Kidney perfusion falls','Renin is released in large amounts','Malignant hypertension and acute kidney injury follow'],
 pearl:'An ACE inhibitor is life-saving here; high-dose steroids can provoke the crisis.'},
{id:'ch_scc',t:'Sunlight to squamous cell carcinoma',sub:['bio','path'],sys:'MSK & skin',c:['skin-cancer','dna-repair'],
 steps:['UVB light strikes epidermal DNA','Neighbouring pyrimidines fuse into dimers','Nucleotide excision repair misses some dimers','C-to-T mutations disable TP53 in keratinocytes','An actinic keratosis forms','Squamous cell carcinoma invades the dermis'],
 pearl:'Xeroderma pigmentosum removes excision repair, so skin cancers appear in childhood; transplant recipients on immunosuppression carry many times the usual SCC risk.'},
{id:'ch_pcosloop',t:'The PCOS loop',sub:['phys','path'],sys:'Repro',c:['pcos','cervix-uterus'],
 steps:['Insulin resistance raises circulating insulin','Insulin lowers SHBG and drives theca cells','Free androgens rise','Androgens stall follicles before one becomes dominant','Without ovulation there is no corpus luteum and no progesterone','Unopposed oestrogen thickens the endometrium'],
 pearl:'Hence the raised endometrial cancer risk; a cyclic progestogen or the combined pill protects. Letrozole is first-line for ovulation induction.'},
{id:'ch_hpvcervix',t:'HPV to cervical cancer',sub:['micro','path'],sys:'Repro',c:['cervix-uterus','p53-rb','sti'],
 steps:['High-risk HPV (16, 18) infects the transformation zone','Viral DNA integrates into the host genome','E6 degrades p53 and E7 frees E2F from Rb','Cells divide past their checkpoints: CIN 1 to CIN 3','Carcinoma breaks through the basement membrane'],
 pearl:'Koilocytes mark HPV infection. Screening finds CIN years before invasion, and vaccination prevents most cases.'},
{id:'ch_mole',t:'A complete hydatidiform mole',sub:['path','phys'],sys:'Repro',c:['trophoblastic','thyroid'],
 steps:['A sperm fertilises an egg that has lost its own nucleus','The paternal genome duplicates to 46,XX','No embryo forms but trophoblast proliferates','Villi swell into grape-like vesicles','hCG climbs very high','hCG stimulates the TSH receptor, causing hyperthyroidism'],
 pearl:'Snowstorm ultrasound, a uterus large for dates and early pre-eclampsia. Follow hCG to zero after evacuation: some complete moles become persistent trophoblastic disease.'},
{id:'ch_tetspell',t:'A tetralogy spell',sub:['phys','path'],sys:'Cardio',c:['congenital-heart'],
 steps:['The outflow septum is displaced forward during development','The child is born with pulmonary stenosis, VSD, overriding aorta and RV hypertrophy','Crying or exertion tightens the RV outflow and lowers systemic resistance','More deoxygenated blood crosses the VSD into the aorta','Cyanosis deepens suddenly','Squatting raises systemic resistance and pushes blood back to the lungs'],
 pearl:'Boot-shaped heart on X-ray; 22q11 deletion is common. In a spell give oxygen, the knee-chest position and morphine.'},
{id:'ch_ldl',t:'From VLDL to foam cell',sub:['bio','pharm'],sys:'Cardio',c:['lipids','atherosclerosis'],
 steps:['The liver secretes VLDL carrying ApoB-100','Lipoprotein lipase strips its triglyceride, leaving IDL','Hepatic lipase turns IDL into LDL','Excess LDL lingers in plasma and enters the arterial intima','LDL is oxidised in the intima','Macrophages take up oxidised LDL by scavenger receptors and become foam cells','Foam cells gather as a fatty streak'],
 pearl:'Statins lower liver cholesterol, so the liver makes more LDL receptors and clears LDL from blood; PCSK9 inhibitors stop the receptors being destroyed.'},
{id:'ch_adpkd',t:'Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease',sub:['path','phys'],sys:'Renal',c:['polycystic-kidney','ckd'],
 steps:['One faulty PKD1 or PKD2 copy is inherited','A second hit in a tubular cell disrupts polycystin','Cilia sense flow poorly and cAMP drives growth and fluid secretion','Cysts enlarge both kidneys over decades','Renin rises and hypertension appears','Kidney function declines toward failure'],
 pearl:'Berry aneurysms, liver cysts and mitral prolapse travel with it. Tolvaptan blocks V₂ receptors, lowering cAMP and slowing cyst growth.'},
{id:'ch_haemochrom',t:'Hereditary haemochromatosis',sub:['phys','path'],sys:'GI & liver',c:['liver-metabolic','diabetes'],
 steps:['Both HFE genes carry C282Y','The liver makes too little hepcidin','Ferroportin on gut cells stays open','Iron is absorbed without limit','Transferrin saturates and iron settles in liver, pancreas, heart, skin and joints','Free iron makes radicals: cirrhosis, diabetes and cardiomyopathy'],
 pearl:'Women present later because menstruation removes iron. Phlebotomy treats it; once cirrhosis forms, the risk of hepatocellular carcinoma remains.'},
{id:'ch_counterreg',t:'Defending against a low glucose',sub:['phys'],sys:'Endocrine',c:['hypoglycaemia','insulin-glucagon'],
 steps:['Blood glucose falls','Insulin secretion switches off','Glucagon and adrenaline are released','The liver breaks down glycogen and makes new glucose','If glucose keeps falling, sweating, tremor and palpitations warn the patient','If it falls further, the brain fails: confusion, seizures, coma'],
 pearl:'β-blockers hide the adrenergic warning, and repeated lows blunt it (hypoglycaemia unawareness). In type 1 diabetes the glucagon response is lost within a few years.'},
{id:'ch_scd',t:'Subacute combined degeneration',sub:['bio','path'],sys:'Neuro',c:['spinal-cord','b12-folate'],
 steps:['Vitamin B12 runs short (pernicious anaemia, metformin, strict veganism)','Methylmalonyl-CoA mutase stalls and methylmalonic acid builds up','Abnormal fatty acids are built into myelin','Dorsal columns and lateral corticospinal tracts demyelinate','Vibration and position sense are lost, with spastic weakness and ataxia'],
 pearl:'Nitrous oxide inactivates B12 and can unmask it. Folate alone corrects the blood count but lets the cord damage go on.'},
/* Chains for the new strata */
{id:'ch_fastfat',t:'Burning fat in a fast',sub:['bio','phys'],sys:'General',c:['fatty-acids','insulin-glucagon'],
 steps:['Low insulin and high glucagon activate hormone-sensitive lipase in fat cells','Free fatty acids travel to the liver on albumin','Fatty acyl-CoA enters mitochondria on the carnitine shuttle','β-oxidation yields acetyl-CoA, NADH and FADH₂','Surplus acetyl-CoA becomes acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate','Brain and muscle burn the ketones'],
 pearl:'In MCAD deficiency β-oxidation stalls for medium chains, so glucose falls with few ketones. Malonyl-CoA from the fed state blocks CPT-1, so synthesis and oxidation never run together.'},
{id:'ch_conjugation',t:'How a plasmid spreads resistance',sub:['micro','bio'],sys:'Immune',c:['antimicrobial-resistance'],
 steps:['A donor bacterium carries a conjugative plasmid with a β-lactamase gene','It extends a sex pilus to a recipient cell','One plasmid strand is nicked and passed across','Each cell makes the complementary strand','The recipient now makes β-lactamase and can pass the plasmid on'],
 pearl:'Transposons can move the gene between plasmid and chromosome; transduction uses phage and transformation uses naked DNA.'},
{id:'ch_aciclovir',t:'How aciclovir works',sub:['pharm','micro'],sys:'Immune',c:['antivirals','herpesviruses'],
 steps:['Aciclovir enters an HSV-infected cell','Viral thymidine kinase adds the first phosphate','Host kinases make aciclovir triphosphate','Viral DNA polymerase builds it into the growing chain','The chain terminates and viral DNA synthesis stops'],
 pearl:'Uninfected cells barely activate it, so toxicity is low (watch crystal nephropathy). Thymidine kinase-deficient mutants resist it; foscarnet needs no activation.'},
{id:'ch_induction',t:'Why low blood solubility speeds induction',sub:['pharm','phys'],sys:'General',c:['general-anaesthetics'],
 steps:['The agent is breathed into the alveoli','It diffuses into pulmonary capillary blood','A poorly soluble agent saturates the blood quickly','Alveolar and arterial partial pressure rise fast','Brain partial pressure catches up with arterial blood','Anaesthesia begins'],
 pearl:'Potency is separate: high oil:gas solubility means a low MAC. Nitrous oxide is fast but weak; its MAC above 100% means it cannot anaesthetise alone.'},
{id:'ch_localblock',t:'How a local anaesthetic blocks a nerve',sub:['pharm','phys'],sys:'General',c:['local-anaesthetics','excitability'],
 steps:['The drug is injected as a salt that is partly uncharged base','The uncharged base crosses the nerve membrane','Inside the cell it takes up a proton and becomes charged','The charged form binds open and inactivated Na⁺ channels from inside','Action potentials fail, small pain fibres first'],
 pearl:'Infected, acidic tissue keeps more drug charged outside the nerve, so the block is weaker. Adrenaline slows absorption and prolongs the block.'},
{id:'ch_sux',t:'Succinylcholine at the end plate',sub:['pharm','phys'],sys:'General',c:['nmj-blockers','nmj'],
 steps:['Succinylcholine binds nicotinic receptors at the end plate','The end plate depolarises and muscles fasciculate','The drug is not broken down by acetylcholinesterase, so depolarisation persists','Neighbouring Na⁺ channels stay inactivated','Flaccid paralysis follows (phase I block)','Plasma pseudocholinesterase ends it within minutes'],
 pearl:'Neostigmine deepens a phase I block. Pseudocholinesterase deficiency leaves patients paralysed for hours. Avoid it after the first day of burns or crush injury: spread-out receptors release dangerous potassium.'},
{id:'ch_opioidod',t:'Opioid overdose',sub:['pharm','phys'],sys:'Neuro',c:['opioids','toxicology'],
 steps:['A large dose of a μ agonist reaches the brainstem','μ receptors hyperpolarise respiratory neurons','The response to rising CO₂ is blunted','Breathing slows and becomes shallow','PaCO₂ rises and hypoxaemia follows','Coma, then apnoea'],
 pearl:'Pinpoint pupils with slow breathing: give naloxone. It is short-acting, so a long-acting opioid such as methadone can re-sedate the patient once it wears off.'},
{id:'ch_maletract',t:'Building male external genitalia',sub:['phys'],sys:'Repro',c:['sex-development','testis'],
 steps:['SRY on the Y chromosome switches on in the bipotential gonad','The gonad becomes a testis','Leydig cells secrete testosterone','5α-reductase in genital tissue converts testosterone to DHT','DHT fuses the urogenital folds and labioscrotal swellings','The penis and scrotum form'],
 pearl:'Sertoli AMH removes the Müllerian ducts; without it a normally virilised boy keeps a uterus. In 5α-reductase deficiency the internal Wolffian structures form, but external genitalia stay ambiguous until puberty.'},
{id:'ch_oocyte',t:'The life of an oocyte',sub:['phys','bio'],sys:'Repro',c:['gametogenesis','menstrual-cycle'],
 steps:['Oogonia multiply by mitosis in the fetal ovary','They enter meiosis I and arrest in prophase I as primary oocytes before birth','After puberty the LH surge lets one oocyte finish meiosis I each cycle','A secondary oocyte and the first polar body form','The secondary oocyte arrests in metaphase II and is ovulated','Fertilisation triggers meiosis II and the second polar body'],
 pearl:'Decades waiting in prophase I may explain why non-disjunction rises with maternal age.'},
{id:'ch_refeeding',t:'Refeeding syndrome',sub:['bio','phys'],sys:'Neuro',c:['eating-disorders'],
 steps:['A starved patient has low total-body phosphate, potassium and magnesium','Carbohydrate feeding begins','Insulin surges','Phosphate, potassium and magnesium move into cells','Serum phosphate crashes and cells run short of ATP','Heart failure, arrhythmia, weakness and seizures follow'],
 pearl:'Start feeding slowly, give thiamine first, and replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium with daily checks.'},
{id:'ch_uncal',t:'Uncal herniation',sub:['path','phys'],sys:'Neuro',c:['herniation','cranial-nerves'],
 steps:['An expanding mass raises supratentorial pressure','The medial temporal lobe (uncus) slides over the tentorial edge','It presses on the ipsilateral oculomotor nerve','The pupil on the side of the mass dilates and fixes','The cerebral peduncle is squeezed, causing contralateral hemiparesis','Midbrain compression brings coma and Duret haemorrhages'],
 pearl:'Pupil fibres run on the outside of CN III, so compression blows the pupil early; a diabetic CN III palsy spares the pupil.'}
);

BANK.PT.push(
{id:'pt_emphysema',t:'Emphysema from smoking',d:'A long-term smoker with centriacinar emphysema, compared with a healthy person.',sub:['phys'],sys:'Resp',c:['copd'],
 p:[['FEV₁/FVC','d','Lost recoil lets airways collapse on expiration'],['Total lung capacity','u','Floppy lungs over-inflate'],['Lung compliance','u','Elastic tissue is destroyed'],['Elastic recoil','d','Elastin is digested'],['DLCO','d','Alveolar surface and capillaries are lost'],['Serum α₁-antitrypsin','n','Levels are normal; smoke inactivates the protein locally']],
 x:'Lost alveolar walls remove recoil and diffusing surface, so the lungs are floppy, large and slow to empty. Smoking-related emphysema has normal α₁-antitrypsin levels; smoke oxidants inactivate the protein in the lung.'},
{id:'pt_gentamicin_renal',t:'Gentamicin in kidney failure',d:'A patient on gentamicin develops acute kidney injury and the dose is not changed.',sub:['pharm'],sys:'General',c:['pharmacokinetics','ribosome-drugs'],
 p:[['Gentamicin clearance','d','It is cleared almost entirely by filtration'],['Half-life','u','t½ = 0.7 × Vd / clearance'],['Steady-state level','u','Steady-state level = dose rate / clearance'],['Time to reach steady state','u','Steady state takes 4–5 half-lives'],['Volume of distribution','n','Set by body water, not kidney function'],['Ototoxicity risk','u','Higher trough levels damage hair cells']],
 x:'Gentamicin is cleared by the kidney, so falling clearance lengthens half-life and raises the level the drug settles at. Time to steady state is 4–5 half-lives, so it lengthens too. Vd depends on body water, not on kidney function.'},
{id:'pt_mcad',t:'MCAD deficiency after a missed feed',d:'A toddler with MCAD deficiency fasts overnight during a viral illness. Compare with a healthy child after the same fast.',sub:['bio'],sys:'General',c:['fatty-acids','hypoglycaemia'],
 p:[['Blood glucose','d','No fat-derived fuel for gluconeogenesis, and glycogen runs out'],['Blood ketones','d','Medium chains cannot yield acetyl-CoA for ketogenesis'],['Urine dicarboxylic acids','u','Omega-oxidation handles the blocked chains'],['C8 acylcarnitine','u','Octanoylcarnitine builds up behind the block'],['Plasma insulin','n','Appropriately suppressed by the low glucose']],
 x:'Medium-chain fats cannot be oxidised, so the liver cannot make ketones or power gluconeogenesis. Insulin is appropriately low in both children, which separates this from an insulinoma.'},
{id:'pt_rifampicin',t:'Rifampicin is added',d:'A woman on warfarin and a combined pill starts rifampicin for TB.',sub:['pharm'],sys:'Immune',c:['antimycobacterials','pharmacokinetics'],
 p:[['Hepatic CYP3A4 activity','u','Rifampicin switches on CYP gene transcription'],['Warfarin level','d','Faster hepatic metabolism'],['INR','d','Less warfarin effect'],['Contraceptive reliability','d','Oestrogen is broken down faster'],['Level of a renally cleared, unmetabolised drug such as lithium','n','Renally cleared without metabolism']],
 x:'Rifampicin induces CYP enzymes over 1–2 weeks, speeding the breakdown of warfarin and oestrogen. A drug that is not metabolised, such as lithium, is unaffected.'},
{id:'pt_volatile',t:'A volatile anaesthetic',d:'Sevoflurane is given to a healthy adult breathing spontaneously.',sub:['pharm','phys'],sys:'General',c:['general-anaesthetics'],
 p:[['Cerebral blood flow','u','Volatiles dilate cerebral vessels'],['Cerebral oxygen consumption','d','Neuronal activity is suppressed'],['Ventilatory response to CO₂','d','Brainstem chemoreceptor drive is blunted'],['Systemic vascular resistance','d','Vascular smooth muscle relaxes'],['Uterine muscle tone','d','Smooth muscle relaxes, a bleeding risk after delivery'],['Bronchial smooth muscle tone','d','Bronchial smooth muscle relaxes']],
 x:'Volatiles dilate cerebral vessels while cutting brain metabolism, so they can raise intracranial pressure. They depress breathing, relax smooth muscle (uterus, bronchi, vessels) and lower blood pressure.'},
{id:'pt_morphine',t:'Morphine',d:'Morphine is given intravenously to a patient with acute pain.',sub:['pharm'],sys:'Neuro',c:['opioids'],
 p:[['Pupil size','d','μ action on the Edinger-Westphal nucleus'],['Respiratory rate','d','The brainstem response to CO₂ is blunted'],['Gut motility','d','μ receptors in the enteric plexus'],['PaCO₂','u','Less ventilation'],['Histamine release (itch, flushing)','u','Direct mast cell degranulation'],['Pain perception','d','Action in the dorsal horn and descending pathways']],
 x:'μ agonism constricts the pupils, slows breathing and the gut, and blunts pain. Morphine also releases histamine from mast cells, which causes itch and flushing.'},
{id:'pt_anorexia',t:'Anorexia nervosa',d:'A 17-year-old with restricting anorexia and a BMI of 15.',sub:['phys'],sys:'Neuro',c:['eating-disorders','menstrual-cycle'],
 p:[['GnRH pulses','d','Low energy and leptin silence the hypothalamus'],['Oestradiol','d','Less gonadotrophin drive'],['Bone density','d','Low oestrogen and high cortisol'],['Heart rate','d','Energy saving and high vagal tone'],['Serum cortisol','u','Starvation is a stress state'],['Free T3','d','Less T4 is converted to T3'],['Serum TSH','n','A sick euthyroid pattern'],['Serum potassium (restricting, no purging)','n','Without purging, electrolytes usually stay normal']],
 x:'Starvation silences GnRH and lowers oestrogen, thinning bone. The body saves energy: slow heart, low T3 with normal TSH (a sick euthyroid pattern) and raised cortisol.'},
{id:'pt_bulimia',t:'Bulimia nervosa with vomiting',d:'A young woman of normal weight who vomits several times a day.',sub:['phys'],sys:'Neuro',c:['eating-disorders','acid-base'],
 p:[['Serum bicarbonate','u','HCl is lost in the vomit'],['Serum potassium','d','Aldosterone from volume loss wastes K⁺'],['Serum chloride','d','Chloride is lost in the vomit'],['Urine chloride','d','The kidney holds chloride in volume depletion'],['Serum amylase (salivary)','u','Swollen salivary glands'],['Body weight','n','Binges and purges roughly balance']],
 x:'Vomiting loses HCl, so bicarbonate rises and chloride falls; low volume drives aldosterone, which wastes potassium. Urine chloride is low in vomiting but high with diuretic misuse. Swollen parotids raise salivary amylase.'},
{id:'pt_hyperventilate',t:'Brief hyperventilation for raised ICP',d:'A ventilated patient with rising intracranial pressure is briefly hyperventilated.',sub:['phys'],sys:'Neuro',c:['herniation'],
 p:[['PaCO₂','d','More CO₂ is blown off'],['Cerebral arteriole diameter','d','Alkalosis around the vessels constricts them'],['Cerebral blood volume','d','Narrower vessels hold less blood'],['Intracranial pressure','d','Less blood volume in the fixed skull'],['Cerebral blood flow','d','Constricted vessels carry less flow'],['Cerebral metabolic rate','n','Metabolism is set by neurons, not by CO₂']],
 x:'Low CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles, shrinking blood volume and pressure within minutes. Flow falls too, which risks ischaemia if continued, and brain metabolism is unchanged. It is only a bridge to definitive treatment.'}
);

